r what hawkBit has no equivalent of: staged artifact uploads and aliases, sw-descriptions, signed set manifests, cancelled rollouts and declared latest-version pins. Keeping them in Postgres, not in memory, is what makes running several replicas safe."],[3064,"hawkBit then hands the device every artifact of its distribution set. A pack with several modules runs several independent installs."],[3315,"hawkBit, from its rollout groups"],[3093,"hawkBit: build a module first"],[3823,"hawkBit's artifact files, with `HAWKBIT_ARTIFACT_STORAGE=fs`"],[2646,"hawkBit's artifact store"],[3926,"hawkBit's artifact store (volume or bucket), `hawkbit` mode"],[5029,"hawkBit's DDI root **as devices reach it** (for example `https://updates.example.com/api/hawkbit-ddi`). It is used to build the download URLs devices receive, and its path becomes the prefix of the DDI download route this service serves."],[3768,"hawkBit's own artifact limits (1 GiB per file, 20 GiB in total) apply unless you use the image of this repository or raise them. The image with the S3 artifact plugin is built from `docker/hawkbit` and is not published to a registry."],[5493,"hawkBit's own DoS filter, which limits how often a device may call it"],[4057,"Health"],[3801,"Health, logging and shutdown"],[346,"Helm"],[3796,"Helm"],[385,"Helm 3.2 or newer"],[5576,"Helm chart and values"],[5597,"Helm migration guide"],[5611,"Helm migration guide: the step-by-step procedure for a Helm-managed release."],[35,"Helm or Fastlane"],[5634,"Helm releases with a filesystem KMS engine or local VA CRL storage"],[5776,"Helm runs the pre-upgrade hooks — the CA-to-KMS migration when enabled, and the database job — before applying any workload, so services never start against an unprepared database. If validation rejects unknown values, fix the values file and re-run."],[3344,"Here a hawkBit action is shown as a workflow state: a scheduled action is `CREATED`, a running one `DOWNLOADING` (hawkBit does not report finer steps), a finished one (or one that finished with a warning) `ACTIVATED`, and a failed, cancelled or cancelling one `TERMINATED`. Repeat step 8 for `dev-002` and `dev-003` (the token comes from each target), then call `rollout` once more for `dev-004`. When every action is finished:"],[542,"Here the engine answers &#x2A;*\"can this identity perform this action on this concrete resource?\"**."],[5639,"High: `helm upgrade` fails"],[5647,"High: `helm upgrade` fails on the old keys"],[5659,"High: enrollments calling the webhook fail"],[5635,"High: KMS and VA start on empty volumes"],[5651,"High: services find no data, or a migration aborts"],[5655,"High: the backend no longer starts with them"],[5643,"High: without a bootstrap principal there is no administrator"],[5618,"Historical device events move from the `devices.events` column to a dedicated `device_events` store with their own endpoints. The events endpoint can also stream events over Server-Sent Events (SSE); to fan events out across service instances, each Device Manager instance binds its own durable AMQP queue."],[1694,"History"],[2793,"Holds"],[2819,"Holds"],[3815,"Holds"],[4929,"Holds"],[2960,"Host port"],[3906,"How"],[2738,"How a batch is sized. Only `percentage` makes `rollout_value` a percentage. Any other value (`fixed`, or `numeric`, which the dashboard sends) makes it a device count"],[2680,"How an update reaches a device (native mode)"],[3661,"How depends on the delivery intent (next table)."],[2561,"How devices connect"],[2563,"How devices connect"],[3631,"How it maps"],[4332,"How it was built"],[640,"How it works"],[768,"How Lamassu works"],[5006,"How long a URL can **start** a download. Between `1s` and 7 days. A transfer already under way is not cut when it expires."],[4832,"How long to wait for in-flight requests after `SIGTERM` or `SIGINT`. Keep it below the termination grace period of the pod (the chart sets 120 s)."],[2730,"How modules relate to the build differs by backend:"],[481,"How policies express access"],[4743,"How the configuration is loaded"],[339,"How the deployment fits together"],[3664,"How the module becomes built"],[2326,"How the responder works in Lamassu"],[5062,"How the service identifies itself to others"],[477,"How the two paths relate"],[2379,"How to check the result"],[5542,"How to report a security vulnerability in Lamassu privately, what to include in a report and which releases receive security fixes."],[1258,"How to request registration"],[1208,"HTTP 200 without a device connection"],[1192,"HTTP 500 with an `err` body on enroll or reenroll"],[5144,"HTTP Basic with `hawkbit.username` and `hawkbit.password`. Use HTTPS."],[2328,"HTTP endpoints"],[4828,"HTTP port. `0` picks a random port. The examples and the chart use `10090`."],[568,"HTTP routes with constraints are stricter. The same principal contributing the policy must also satisfy the attributes required by the route. Lamassu does not combine one principal's policy with an attribute belonging to another."],[485,"HTTP rules"],[577,"HTTP rules do not point directly at tables. They reference a route schema and grant its logical actions. Job Manager's policies are an example: they distinguish reading, creating, updating and deleting workflows or jobs."],[525,"HTTP rules primarily answer &#x2A;*\"can this identity call this API operation?\"**. On their own they do not produce filters over the external service's database. To limit a route to a specific device, tenant or client, either the schema declares a constraint relating a request value to a normalized attribute of the principal, or the policy pins the request value to a literal with `param_constraints`. The first scopes each identity to its own resources through a single grant; the second restricts a grant to a fixed value regardless of who holds it."],[5287,"HTTP status codes"],[1362,"HTTP URL of Lamassu's CA service."],[1358,"HTTP URL of Lamassu's Device Manager."],[1354,"HTTP URL of Lamassu's DMS Manager service."],[404,"I cannot sign in