o key at build time, the build still succeeds and the pack records the gap. If **no** device has a key, the build fails instead."],[1701,"If an expiration or renewal state appears, check certificate dates and DMS thresholds in Devices and identities."],[1700,"If assignment fails, review permissions and operation diagnostics before repeating it. Query the device and its history again to find the persisted result."],[2435,"If creation fails, check permissions, engine availability and the selected algorithm."],[1632,"If curl fails, use Troubleshooting with the saved body. Do not convert it or repeat the request without querying inventory: a timeout or error can follow persisted issuance."],[322,"If data planes and TLS termination must stay independent behind one IP, add a front-door SNI passthrough layer."],[320,"If Gateways need independent policy and you can allocate IPs, use one VIP per Gateway."],[2154,"If generation fails (for example, because of a role without a CA or an invalid signer), the job logs the warning `something went wrong while calculating CRL for CA <ski>` and retries on the next cycle. There is no other renewal path until the cause is fixed, and the last published list keeps being served until it expires. All of this depends on working jobs, storage and signing."],[2893,"If it applies"],[3108,"If it is empty, the dashboard uses `LAMASSU_API`."],[228,"If Keycloak is external and already has its own reachable URL, point the OIDC settings at it and omit the `/auth` entry from `gateway.extraRouting`."],[425,"If login succeeds but the APIs return 403:"],[1931,"If neither is supplied"],[549,"If no combination of principal, policy and rule allows the operation, Lamassu denies it, unless the HTTP schema declares `skip_authz` on the route or `default_action: allow` on a base path that covers it (see *Authentication-only routes and unmapped paths*). Additionally, `auth.externalAuthorization.failOpen` is `false` by default: if `authz` is unavailable, the Gateway blocks protected routes."],[1699,"If no eligible certificate appears, check that its `Common Name` matches the `Device ID` exactly, it is active and it was issued by the expected CA."],[323,"If none of those are possible, use different external ports or change the external network design."],[2896,"If not, nothing more happens"],[3432,"If one device of the first batch fails (`TERMINATED`) and the other succeeds, 50% succeeded meets the threshold and the next batch is dispatched on its own."],[319,"If one Gateway can own TLS and routing, use the chart default."],[321,"If only the Kubernetes resources need separation, evaluate merged-Gateway mode and make every listener tuple unique."],[2436,"If OpenSSL cannot read the file, copy the complete PEM block again."],[5554,"If private vulnerability reporting is not enabled for that repository, report the vulnerability privately to lamassuiot\\@lksnext.com before disclosing any details publicly."],[2437,"If signature or date checks fail, check that you downloaded this CA's certificate and that your computer's clock is correct."],[2888,"If some jobs cannot be created, those devices stay `pending` and the call returns a \"partial rollout\" error (`500`)."],[2877,"If the build fails, the pack becomes `build_failed`, `last_build_error` says why, and the pack stays editable. The selected artifacts replace the pack version's earlier artifact links."],[3415,"If the build fails, the pack gets the status `build_failed` and `last_build_error` holds the reason. It stays editable: fix the cause and build again."],[5760,"If the deployment runs on SQLite, `couch_db` or `dynamo_db`, move it to PostgreSQL first."],[5777,"If the deployment uses local KMS or VA storage, those pods fail until you complete step 4, so `--wait` cannot succeed: Helm waits for them to become ready, times out, exits with an error and marks the release `failed`. The hooks have already run by then, and every manifest, including the new StatefulSets and their PVCs, has been applied. Helm does not roll anything back without `--atomic`. Continue with step 4, then re-run the same `helm upgrade` command so it completes and Helm records the release as `deployed`. Both hooks are idempotent, so they are safe to run again."],[3066,"If the device verifies signatures, set its public key (`public-key-file` in SWUpdate's configuration) and sign the package with the matching key. If it decrypts, it needs the same key and algorithm the pack was built with. A mismatch is the usual reason an install fails silently after a successful download. Signing and encryption explains which key, algorithm and IV each mode uses, and what the device receives."],[2151,"If the first CA is deleted, CRL generation for the remaining one fails because the configured signer no longer matches a CA with that name. Until the role’s signer is updated to a certificate of the remaining CA, the last published list keeps being served until it expires (7 days by default)."],[2047,"If the identity stops being trustworthy, Lamassu records the reason and time of revocation. OCSP and CRLs let consumers learn that status."],[2462,"If the key must be generated and kept on the destination system, use **Upload Existing CSR**, described in the authorities guide."],[2259,"If the key will be born inside Lamassu, the recommended flow is to generate a new pair managed directly by one of the configured engines."],[3553,"If the pack has preconditions, preview who qualifies before creating anything by adding `?dry_run=true` to the same request. It answers `200` with `qualifying_devices`, `precondition_failures`, `superseded_devices` and `never_seen_devices`, and creates nothing."],[3206,"If the pack has preconditions, the dashboard first runs a dry run and shows **Campaign preconditions**: how many devices qualify, who does not and why. Tick &#x2A;*Force deploy to non-qualifying devices (not recommended)** to send to them anyway, then **Confirm campaign**."],[971,"If the previous certificate is ACTIVE, attempts Superseded revocation before binding its successor."],[1139,"If the previous identity is already revoked, retaining its files does not restore a valid connection. Recovery requires an admitted credential and authorized intervention according to the situation."],[1661,"If the result fails"],[2412,"If the result fails"],[2452,"If the result fails"],[522,"If the route declares constraints, it compares request data with the normalized attributes of the same principal contributing the policy; the policy's own `param_constraints` also pin the granted action to literal request values."],[1940,"If the selected identifier cannot be resolved, the operation fails; it does not silently fall back to the CA’s profile. Avoid setting both options at once. Recording which profile was selected helps explain why a certificate differs from its CSR."],[1430,"If the Thing already existed in AWS IoT Core, the connector iterates all certificates (principals) currently attached to that Thing and marks them as `REVOKED` in AWS, ensuring previous certificates cannot keep being used to authenticate."],[829,"If unavailable, **Issue New Instead** starts issuance using the intended CA. Review the profile first."],[283,"If users connect by DNS name, put that name in `tls.certManagerOptions.certSpec.hostnames`. Do not copy an upstream proxy's public IP into `gateway.addresses`."],[1986,"If using both, define when fallback is allowed and what the client does when retrieval, signature or freshness checks fail, or the result is `Unknown`. Accepting an identity when its status cannot be checked must be an explicit application decision."],[1722,"If you are still preparing the instance, start in Deployment. The Platform paths assume an accessible console and permission to perform the operations."],[3518,"If you build the `.swu` yourself and upload it as a prebuilt deliverable, you fill in `sha256` and `ivt` yourself, and the `sha256` is over the bytes the device downloads, because SWUpdate hashes before it decrypts."],[1698,"If you cannot select the DMS, confirm its creation and your read permissions."],[2464,"If you continue with manual registration, the `Device ID` will be exactly `device-001`."],[1916,"If you enable **Honor Key Usage** or **Honor Extended Key Usages**, Lamassu keeps the values requested by the CSR. If you disable them, it replaces them with the ones defined in the profile."],[5767,"If you kept the 3.8.0 role defaults (`realm_access.roles` and `pki-admin`), the default `services.authz.bootstrap` already matches them."],[731,"If you need retention, search, tamper protection or regulatory export, connect a consumer that persists the events into the system approved by your organization."],[420,"If you receive a 401:"],[1218,"If you receive an identity, check the key, subject, use and connection with the consumer."],[457,"if you set `amqp.ca.secretName`, confirm the Secret exists in the release namespace and its data key matches `amqp.ca.certificateKey` (default `ca.crt`); if `amqp.ca.secretName` is not set, the clients trust the system CAs;"],[2471,"If you use an intermediate CA, the trusted root must be in `ca.pem` and you must supply the intermediates with `-untrusted intermediates.pem`. This variant is covered in the trust model."],[3403,"If you use the hawkBit backend the flow differs, mainly in how a pack is built and how devices report. For the screens that do the same, see the dashboard guide."],[229,"If your values file still carries the legacy gateway JWT validation settings — `auth.oidc.apiGateway.jwks`, `auth.authorization.rolesClaim` or `auth.authorization.roles.admin` — remove them. The migration guide `charts/lamassu/CHANGELOG/3.8.0->4.0.0.md` documents their removal with no compatibility aliases, and `values.schema.json` rejects the leftover blocks instead of silently ignoring them. The replacements are `services.authz.jwkUrl` and `services.authz.bootstrap`, already shown in the example above."],[1008,"Implement renewal and recovery and trust updates. The EST reference covers endpoints, formats and ServerKeyGen; troubleshooting helps distinguish transport, admission and persistence failures."],[2728,"import a module that another pack already has."],[2213,"Import an existing pair"],[1822,"Import an external CA"],[4377,"Import body: `source_module_id`, or `source_distribution_set_name` and `module_key` (with optional `source_group_id` and `source_distribution_set_version`). A module's delivery intent must be decided and compatible with the pack's packaging before it can be imported."],[2227,"Import keys created externally."],[1868,"Import the certificate and, when applicable, its PEM key. Register the CAs needed to identify the issuer. Registering a CA does not install it in external trust stores."],[2733,"Importing a module also differs. In hawkBit mode the import **links** it, so both packs share the module and a rebuild is seen by both. In native mode it **copies** it, so the two diverge afterwards. The listing of reusable modules tells you which one you will get (`shared`)."],[2266,"Importing lets you register in Lamassu a key generated outside the platform. It is the usual flow in BYOK scenarios, migrations or integration with cryptographic material already in production."],[2419,"In **Certification Authorities**, select **Create New CA** and choose to create a CA with a new key pair."],[1847,"In **Certification Authorities**, select **Create New CA** and choose to generate a new key pair."],[1671,"In **Enrollment CA**, select the CA that issued `device.crt`."],[1672,"In **Registration Mode**, select `PRE_REGISTRATION` for the path that starts with explicit registration."],[1387,"In `auto` mode: creates/updates policies, ensures Thing groups, revokes stale principals and provisions the Thing via `iot:RegisterThing`. In `jitp` mode: no AWS calls; AWS runs the JITP template on first connection."],[5221,"In `hawkbit` mode `POST .../swu` returns `501`. Send the same signing and encryption fields to the module build instead: `POST /updates/v1/softwaremodules/by-id/:moduleID/build` (for `swu-build` and `raw` modules). See hawkBit mode."],[3763,"In `hawkbit` mode a module build replaces the module's input files with the deliverable, so a module cannot be built twice: release a new version."],[3744,"In `hawkbit` mode nothing is elected and there are no local files to share, so any number of replicas can run: state is in Postgres and hawkBit, and module and launch operations take Postgres locks. If you use shared-key encryption, put SymKMS key storage (`symkms.storage`) on shared storage or S3. See Operations."],[5123,"In `hawkbit` mode the DDI artifact route serves a file only if the module is assigned to or installed on that device."],[5052,"In `hawkbit` mode the SymKMS routes are registered only when `symkms.storage.type` or `sym_keys_dir` is set. With `symkms_endpoint` set but neither of those, every key lookup would be answered `404` by the service itself."],[3898,"In `hawkbit` mode there is nothing to elect and no local files to share. The chart still mounts the `ReadWriteOnce` volume while `persistence.enabled` is true, so set it to `false` to run replicas on different nodes."],[3761,"In `hawkbit` mode, a pack with several modules installs as several independent SWUpdate transactions. SWUpdate cannot roll back across several `.swu` files."],[5540,"In `hawkbit` mode, inspect what the service created through hawkBit's Management API (port `8090` in Compose, with the Basic Auth credentials of the configuration). Each hawkBit instance has its own data: query the one named in `hawkbit.base_url`."],[3638,"In `hawkbit` mode, Lamassu Updates serves the same `/updates/v1` API on top of an Eclipse hawkBit server instead of its own Postgres, WFX and SymKMS-based backend. The service becomes an **adapter**: hawkBit owns packs, modules, rollouts and jobs and is read live on every request, and devices talk to hawkBit directly."],[1460,"In `jitp` mode the connector makes no provisioning calls: AWS executes the JITP template on the device's first connection and creates the Thing, registers the certificate and activates it on its own."],[1381,"In `jitp` mode, attempts to create the provisioning template and attaches it to the registered CA. Non-idempotent: a `PUT /v1/dms/{id}` edit retries the creation and lands in `lamassu-dlq` after 3 retries. Idempotent for `none` and `auto` (configuration only)."],[5120,"In `native` mode, pack, group and device identifiers used in paths are validated so they cannot escape the storage directories, and a per-device pack never falls back to the shared build for a device."],[5712,"In 3.8.0 `amqp.tls` was not applied and event-bus clients always connected over plaintext. In 4.0.0 `amqp.tls: true` switches them to AMQPS. The default `amqp.port` stays `5672` and does not change when TLS is enabled, so a values file that already set `amqp.tls: true` must point `amqp.port` at the broker's TLS listener (`amqps`, commonly `5671`), or set `amqp.tls: false` to keep plaintext. Clients must also trust the broker's certificate."],[5616,"In 3.8.0 the API Gateway validated tokens: a JWT policy on every private route checked the signature and only let through tokens that carried the administrator role. Every route was open to any token that passed that check."],[1053,"In a Bash session with `set -euo pipefail` and `umask 077`, use `EST_BASE` and the trust/bootstrap files from EST client. Select an available and admitted `est-serverkeygen-001`. The `server-request.key` only signs the input CSR; the received identity uses the server-generated key."],[1635,"In a second terminal, enter the same directory and run `umask 077`. Create a local server certificate and start a test consumer. This server trusts `device-root.pem` and requires a valid client identity:"],[767,"In a self-managed deployment, Lamassu uses PostgreSQL for persistence, RabbitMQ for messaging and an OIDC provider for authentication. See the deployment architecture for how the services are published on Kubernetes."],[2473,"In Bash, compare the two public-key digests:"],[3175,"In hawkBit mode a pack's files live in modules, built in the catalog."],[3580,"In hawkBit mode a rollout can only be paused after it has started dispatching."],[3060,"In hawkBit mode devices do not poll Lamassu Updates. They poll hawkBit's DDI API, and Lamassu Updates reads the resulting action state from hawkBit."],[4145,"In hawkBit mode no list is paged (`next` is always empty). Only the fleet-wide `/devices/distribution-sets` and `/devices/distribution-set-updates/latest` apply `filter`."],[3155,"In hawkBit mode the form has one more section, **Software modules**. A hawkBit set links built modules, so tick at least one built module there, and at most one OS module. The table lists only modules that fit the **Driver**: an SWU set hides modules delivered as raw files, and a Generic set hides modules delivered as a prebuilt SWU. If nothing fits, it says \"No built module fits this driver\", and you build one first (see hawkBit: build a module first)."],[4583,"In hawkBit mode the service also serves hawkBit's device artifact URL, so a gateway can publish it on the same host as the rest of the API:"],[2574,"In hawkBit mode the service is a gateway. Packs become distribution sets, launches become rollouts, and hawkBit owns that data and is read live on every request. The service keeps in Postgres only what hawkBit has no place for. See Data model."],[3552,"In hawkBit mode there is no WFX. The choice is hawkBit's install mode."],[2817,"In hawkBit mode, packs, rollouts and jobs live in hawkBit. This service keeps only the following in the same Postgres (the `updates` database), for the things hawkBit has no equivalent of:"],[1461,"In JITP mode the device also performs its EST enrollment conventionally and obtains its certificate from Lamassu. The difference is that AWS IoT Core does not know the device until it attempts its first connection."],[1634,"In Lamassu, open `est-device-001`. It must be created with the intended owning DMS, a current identity and the received certificate in its history. Compare the local serial with the slot; thresholds can show a renewal state rather than simply Active."],[2083,"In Lamassu, trust does not simply mean that a certificate exists in the inventory. A consumer trusts an identity when it can build a chain to a root it recognizes, check that the certificate is fit for the intended use and verify that it is still valid."],[1042,"In mTLS mode, the enrollment CA always validates the re-enrollment credential; certificates from other issuers are accepted only if they are listed as validation CAs in the re-enrollment block. The expiry check through `enable_expired_renewal` belongs to that validator. A revoked current certificate and a request before the window are rejected. See Renewal for installation and recovery."],[3027,"In native mode a launch creates one WFX job per device. The device polls WFX for jobs addressed to its ID, downloads the deliverable from the Updates API, installs it and reports each state back to WFX. Lamassu Updates reads those states from WFX to drive the launch."],[4734,"In native mode a launch dispatches one job per device with a single download URI, so a module has no deliverable of its own: the pack builds one `.swu` from every module's inputs. That is why `software_module_deliverables` and `software_module_replace` are unsupported there."],[2570,"In native mode the service owns the data. Postgres holds packs, launches and device history, WFX holds one job per device, and the service drives both."],[2225,"In practice, it lets you:"],[5240,"In SymKMS, `POST /symkms/v1/` with `user_id`, an `algorithm` from the table and a `key` (hex or base64) of the right length."],[633,"In the `authz` logs, an external endpoint decision includes `allowed`, `reason`, `matched_principals`, `evaluated_policy_ids`, `matched_policy_id` and the status code. These fields let you separate a matching failure from a permissions failure. With OpenTelemetry tracing enabled, a request that reaches the policy engine produces an `engine.CheckHTTPRequest` span whose attributes record `authz.allowed`, the matched policy and the matched principal. Correlation with the Gateway trace requires trace context to be propagated to authz."],[5502,"In the `phased` workflow some steps are made by WFX or an operator"],[1673,"In the authentication section, enable **Require client certificate** and leave **Call external webhook** off. Under **Require client certificate**, add the CA whose initial certificates you will accept to **Validation CAs**; for this evaluation, it can be the same CA. Keep **Verify CSR Signature** enabled and leave **Enable server-side key generation** off if you will not use it."],[2223,"In the current configuration, Lamassu IoT works exclusively with asymmetric keys, such as RSA and elliptic curves. These keys are used to issue X.509 certificates, sign requests and perform authentication and validation operations within the system."],[3136,"In the dashboard, a pack is a **distribution set** and a launch is a **campaign**."],[372,"In the default `tls.type: certManager` mode, cert-manager `Issuer` and CA `Certificate` resources and a chart-managed downstream `Certificate`; in the `tls.type: external` mode, a reference to your TLS Secret"],[2090,"In the device-generated CSR flow, the CA applies the profile and signs without receiving the device’s private key."],[1247,"In the DMS, separate distributed CAs from the re-enrollment validation CAs at `reenrollment_settings.est_rfc7030_settings.client_certificate_settings.validation_cas`, used for re-enrollment credentials. That list is optional because the enrollment CA always validates re-enrollment credentials, but when switching the enrollment CA, add the previous issuance CA to it so old-CA identities keep re-enrolling during overlap. The inspected validator tries