"],[2517,"Its public key matches the one included in `device.crt`."],[328,"Its status contains the expected address."],[2394,"its subject and extensions identify the expected client;"],[2702,"Job"],[2630,"Job orchestration"],[3951,"Job orchestration. Each device update is a WFX job that the device polls"],[2704,"job, device assignment"],[4042,"Jobs"],[3665,"Joins packs with"],[4054,"JSON, except uploads, which are `multipart/form-data` with the file in the field `file`"],[25,"Just want to evaluate the platform?"],[1434,"Just-In-Time Provisioning (JITP) mode delegates provisioning to AWS instead of the connector doing it directly. When the connector detects the DMS is in `jitp` mode, it creates a JITP provisioning template in AWS IoT Core and attaches it to the registered CA certificate. The connector attempts to create this template when the DMS is created and again on every full DMS edit (`PUT /v1/dms/{id}`) — not at device enrollment time. The attempt is not idempotent: when a template named after the DMS ID already exists, the creation call fails, the event is retried three times and finally lands in the `lamassu-dlq` poison queue (see Troubleshooting). The template is created once; later DMS edits never update its content. A DMS update that omits `metadata` or sends `null` keeps the stored metadata, so the connector key `lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}` remains present and the attempt is still triggered. A body that supplies a `metadata` object replaces the stored metadata entirely: if that object does not carry the connector key, the key is dropped and the connector silently skips the DMS's future events. Once the template is attached, when a device with a certificate issued by that CA connects to AWS IoT Core for the first time, AWS executes the template automatically: it creates the Thing, registers the certificate and activates it."],[265,"K3s"],[286,"K3s ServiceLB implements each `LoadBalancer` Service with pods that reserve the Service ports on the node. On 