ion, issuer and timestamps with the copy downloaded from the public endpoint. Diagnose differences using PKI troubleshooting."],[1857,"The CA's internal identifier is generated automatically and cannot be changed."],[1890,"The CA’s VA role is kept. If no other CA with the same SKI remains, it can no longer be edited (see CRL), so adjust its validity and signer beforehand. The CA’s key is released, but the KMS keeps it while other certificates still reference it."],[3253,"The campaign is cancelled once this % of all its devices have failed"],[3254,"The campaign stops when more than this % of a batch fails"],[5403,"The canary check runs before the group's devices exist in hawkBit"],[3466,"The canary failed. Resume the launch to retry it, or cancel"],[3370,"The canary fails with `device ... does not exist`"],[3424,"The canary passed. Each `rollout` call now sends the next batch of two:"],[4494,"The canary: it goes alone first and must succeed before the rest"],[3269,"The capabilities request succeeded and the backend does not support them. Check **Updates backend** in the user menu (development builds) or `GET /updates/v1/capabilities`"],[3191,"The catalog also works on the native backend: it lists the modules of every pack, and **New module** can leave a module in **Catalog only — no set** until you add it to a pack with **Import module**. Native import copies the module, while hawkBit linking shares it."],[1189,"The certificate and key do not form a matching pair. Enter both values from the same pair; the check runs when the DMS is saved and again on every webhook call."],[332,"The certificate contains the DNS name or IP used by the client."],[2045,"The certificate enters the `ACTIVE` state. Lamassu monitors its `Not After` date; the cryptographic monitoring job marks certificates that have expired as `EXPIRED`."],[2386,"The certificate's purpose, such as authenticating a client through mTLS."],[2150,"The certificates of all of them point to `/crl/{ski}`, but the published list has the first CA as issuer and only includes its revocations. A verifier compares the CRL issuer with the issuer of the certificate it validates (RFC 5280, §6.3.2) and rejects the list if they differ. Depending on its policy, the certificate is blocked (*hard-fail*) or accepted even though it is revoked (*soft-fail*). Revocations of the secondary CAs do not appear in any list."],[2526,"The chain validates but the connection fails"],[462,"The chart can enable OpenTelemetry instrumentation for the services that support it, including authz; it does not configure OpenTelemetry for AWS Connector, wfx or the UI. Traces are exported over OTLP HTTP and logs can be directed to a compatible endpoint, such as VictoriaLogs. Use a correlation identifier and the same time window to join Gateway, service and dependency."],[380,"The chart creates Lamassu's `Gateway` and routes. It does not install the Envoy Gateway controller or make the generated `LoadBalancer` Service reachable from your network."],[382,"The chart creates one shared `Gateway` per Helm release. All chart-managed `HTTPRoute` resources attach to it. Envoy Gateway provisions the proxy data plane and a Kubernetes `LoadBalancer` Service for that Gateway."],[220,"The chart defaults to a `GatewayClass` named `eg`. Change `gateway.className` if your platform team uses another name."],[222,"The chart has no Helm dependencies that install PostgreSQL, RabbitMQ, an OIDC provider, Envoy Gateway or cert-manager."],[3839,"The chart has no ingress template: `ingress.*` in `values.yaml` has no effect. Expose the service with your own ingress or gateway."],[596,"The chart includes a bootstrap principal that looks for the OIDC role `pki-admin` in `realm_access.roles`. It grants it `SUPER ADMIN` and, when Job Manager is enabled, the administrative policy of its NBI API."],[3827,"The chart is `helm/updates-monolithic` in the repository. It deploys the `updates` service only: Postgres, WFX, Lamassu and, in `hawkbit` mode, hawkBit are external."],[234,"The chart mounts configured Secrets read-only into every AMQP-consuming workload: the CA certificate under `/etc/lamassu/amqp/ca` and the client certificate and key under `/etc/lamassu/amqp/client`. The mounted file names are always `ca.crt`, `tls.crt` and `tls.key` — the `certificateKey` and `privateKeyKey` values rename only the Secret data keys, never the on-disk paths. The client private key is mounted with restrictive permissions (`defaultMode: 0440`; the CA volume uses `0444`), which works because the chart adds `fsGroup` to the pod security context when `amqp.client.secretName` is set — it uses the container `runAsGroup` (default `65532`) when `fsGroup` is not already set."],[3863,"The chart renders the whole `config` value, including `storage.password`, `encryption.local_key`, S3 keys and `hawkbit.password`, into a ConfigMap. It has no `existingSecret` option, and no value to add environment variables or volumes, so the service's environment-variable overrides and its `*_file` credentials cannot be fed from a Kubernetes Secret without patching or wrapping the chart. Until then:"],[419,"The chart's services expose startup, readiness and liveness probes over /health. A pod can be running but receive no traffic if the readiness probe fails."],[1068,"The client requests an identity for its `Device ID` through EST."],[1044,"The code supports RSA and ECDSA, and the `bits` value of the DMS key setting means something different for each. For ECDSA it selects the curve: 224, 256, 384 or 521 map to P-224, P-256, P-384 and P-521, and an unrecognized size falls back to P-256, so explicitly select a compatible size and check the resulting key. For RSA it is used directly as the modulus size; the console offers 2048, 3072 and 4096 bits. Do not reuse an enrolled ID to test this operation without reviewing replacement."],[2472,"The command does not query OCSP or CRL. For revocation checks, continue with Certificate validation. See the openssl-verify reference for its options."],[466,"the commands or queries used;"],[259,"The complete values reference is maintained in `charts/lamassu/VALUES.md` in the Helm repository."],[2559,"The components of Lamassu Updates, how they connect in each backend, and what runs in the background."],[464,"the confirmed cause;"],[3757,"The connection to Postgres always uses `sslmode=disable`. In `native` mode the service does not verify the TLS certificate of the KMS, and the SWU signer script calls `curl -k`."],[4863,"The connection uses `sslmode=disable`. There is no way to enable TLS towards Postgres from the configuration. Keep the database on a private network."],[1498,"The connector can write to the AWS IoT Core Device Shadow to send action signals to the device firmware. This feature is enabled per DMS with `shadow_config.enable: true`."],[1505,"The connector creates and attaches the policies configured in the DMS. A typical policy for a device using MQTT and the Device Shadow has this structure:"],[1435,"The connector creates the template, named after the DMS ID, only once. Each later edit of the DMS that keeps the connector key tries to create it again and fails, because the connector does not update existing templates. Later changes to `jitp_config.provisioning_role_arn`, `jitp_config.enable_template` or to the groups and policies the template references must be applied by hand in AWS IoT Core."],[1281,"The connector does not poll Lamassu's services periodically. Instead, it **subscribes to Lamassu's internal event bus** (AMQP or AWS SQS/SNS, configurable) and reacts to the events it cares about: CA creation or update, DMS creation or update, device identity binding, certificate status change, certificate metadata update and device metadata update. This event-driven model ensures synchronization with AWS is nearly immediate and produces no polling load."],[1419,"The connector internally generates an ephemeral RSA-2048 key pair and builds a CSR whose Common Name is exactly that registration code. It then calls Lamassu's signing service so the registered CA issues a real X.509 verification certificate signing that CSR. The ephemeral private key is used only for this purpose and is neither stored nor sent to AWS."],[1468,"The connector listens for the `EventUpdateCertificateStatusKey` event and, when a certificate holding the connector's metadata key changes status in Lamassu, replicates that change in AWS IoT Core. The following table shows the status mapping:"],[1363,"The connector owns the AWS side of four resource types — CAs, DMS provisioning templates (JITP), device certificates and Things. This section maps the bus events the connector consumes to the AWS resources they touch and to the AWS calls each flow performs. The state machine of each resource lives next to its detailed explanation; the last column links to it. AWS resources are referenced with the notation `IoTCore/<resource>`."],[1500,"The connector writes to the `desired.identity_actions` field of the shadow document. Each entry contains a Unix timestamp in milliseconds. The device firmware must read this field and act accordingly."],[763,"The console and the APIs manage authorities, certificates, DMSs, devices, alerts and configuration. Domain events allow connectors and automations to react to changes without continuously polling the platform."],[673,"The console evaluates the filter against the JSON shown in the **Input Event** editor, which is prefilled with the latest event, and reports whether it matches. For JSON Path and JavaScript filters, it shows **The filter matches this Cloud Event** or **The filter does not match this Cloud Event**. JSON Path expressions are evaluated against the full Cloud Event, so the default `$.data` selects the event's `data` field, and the expression must start with `$`."],[2398,"the consumer applies revocation checks and authorization."],[2087,"The consumer keeps its own trust store. EST `/cacerts` can distribute the service TLS