ssions in the consumer."],[1036,"These routes do not implement `/csrattrs` or `/fullcmc`. There is no native JWT EST authentication mode; a webhook can interpret a request credential according to its own contract."],[1676,"These settings prepare DMS policy. The next steps register and assign through the console; they do not send an EST request or test admission through that protocol."],[547,"They are not two equivalent controls applied twice. HTTP rules protect the perimeter of an integrated service; entity rules express permissions over the data model of the native services."],[1268,"Thing lifecycle"],[1388,"Thing lifecycle"],[1453,"Things only exist under `auto` mode; in `jitp` mode AWS creates the Thing itself when the device first connects. The Thing name is always the device certificate's CN:"],[3294,"Things that behave differently"],[2872,"Things to know about the build:"],[1080,"This block describes `main` code inspected at commit `8f42c47157f9a36bac3ed5caa3692aebebe19c4b`. The site catalog publishes `main (unstable)`; this behavior is not attributed to a stable release. Before using another version, check its contract, particularly independent re-enrollment authentication. Client checks and live-instance tests are distinguished in the revision plan."],[285,"This design uses one IP, one TLS entry point and one pair of ports. The chart already creates the required routes."],[1279,"This document is aimed both at the PKI administrator, who needs to configure the connector and register CAs, and at the device firmware developer, who needs to understand how Things are named, how the Device Shadow works and what happens when a certificate is revoked. For the firmware developer, the most important fact is that **the AWS Thing name (ThingName) always corresponds to the Common Name (CN) of the certificate issued by Lamassu**, regardless of the provisioning mode."],[2027,"This does not undo permanent revocation or a cascade started by revoking a CA."],[2360,"This example omits the nonce because the responder does not echo it. Verification relies on signature, response correspondence and timestamps; it does not demonstrate freshness through a nonce."],[563,"This example recognizes certificates whose CN starts with `factory-a-` and that are signed by the configured CA:"],[3430,"This example uses a second pack, `valve-panel`, created and built like `water-panel` (steps 2 to 4). Artifact names are global and a name that already exists must get a higher version, so give this one its own file name:"],[2307,"This flow is useful for:"],[1863,"This flow is useful when the key was previously created in an HSM, imported through BYOK or must be reused under a specific custody policy."],[1496,"This forced disconnect happens at the moment Lamassu records the status change. If the device tries to reconnect after the disconnect, AWS IoT Core will reject the connection because the certificate is already listed as `REVOKED` or `INACTIVE`."],[1959,"This guide brings together the checks needed when integrating a certificate consumer. You need the presented certificate, intermediates and roots that your application has deliberately chosen to trust. Lamassu publishes status; the consumer applies acceptance policy."],[848,"This guide connects the DMS administrator with device manufacturing and integration. Before requesting an operational identity, the client must authenticate the EST server and establish entitlement to request the intended ID."],[3022,"This guide describes the contract between a device and Lamassu Updates, for each backend. The device software is SWUpdate: this page covers what the device must send and receive, not SWUpdate's own installation and configuration, which its documentation describes. Use it when a launch is running and no device moves."],[2527,"This guide helps a PKI operator locate issuance and status publication failures. Keep the Lamassu version, operation, CA, issuer, serial, time and returned error. Do not include private keys or tokens in diagnostics."],[1089,"This guide helps the provisioning administrator choose a path before configuring the DMS. Separate three decisions: when the device exists, how its request is authorized and who generates its key. None grants destination access by itself."],[798,"This guide is for a fleet operator. You need device, DMS and certificate permissions for the intended actions. A fleet is organized through these resources, tags and metadata; there is no separate “fleet” object to create."],[2007,"This guide is for a PKI operator inspecting or retiring an issued identity. You need inventory access and, to change states, permissions on the certificate. Open **Certificates** for a global view or **Issued Certificates** within a CA to restrict it to that issuer."],[1228,"This guide is for device and consumer trust administrators. You need issuer inventory, trust-store access and an authenticated update channel. Distributing certificates is separate from choosing trusted anchors."],[1911,"This guide is for the PKI administrator defining issuance policy. You need permissions to manage profiles and associate them with the intended CA or DMS, plus a test consumer with known identity and purpose requirements."],[2232,"This guide is for the PKI operator: select and use an already enabled engine. Installation, credentials, persistent storage and connectivity belong to Deployment. Before generating a CA, check the default engine, advertised algorithms and a test signature. Availability in your installation depends on its version and configuration."],[995,"This guide is for those implementing the device client. The administrator must have prepared the DMS policy. For a complete first run, follow Enroll and connect your first device."],[2949,"This guide starts Lamassu Updates (the OTA Update Service) with Docker Compose, in its default native mode, and checks that the API answers. It takes about ten minutes plus the image build. When it works, continue with P