d data."],[2392,"Validation covers the chain, validity, usages and revocation status. Lamassu publishes status through OCSP and CRL; the consumer configures which issuers it recognizes and how it checks that status."],[468,"validation from a consumer;"],[727,"validation functions and CRL;"],[1895,"Validity"],[2484,"Validity fails"],[3828,"Value"],[3888,"Value"],[2835,"Values worth knowing:"],[5036,"Variable"],[5035,"Variables the code reads besides these overrides:"],[3390,"Variations"],[2218,"Verify"],[3807,"Verify"],[1244,"Verify chain, dates, names, usages and revocation from the consumer."],[1607,"Verify CSR Signature"],[422,"verify issuer, audience and expiration of the token;"],[450,"verify it is enabled in the emitting service;"],[429,"verify that authz reaches PostgreSQL and its JWKS."],[2944,"Verify the API"],[2450,"Verify the chain"],[1216,"Verify the CSR locally and relate its CN to the inventory."],[1659,"Verify the device"],[434,"Verify the DNS and port configured in externalAuthorization."],[872,"Verify the endpoints"],[1826,"Verify the import"],[89,"Verify the installation"],[1586,"Verify the obtained identity"],[793,"Verify the registration"],[2013,"Verify the request with `openssl req -in request.csr -noout -verify -text`. Review subject, SAN and algorithm, and authorize the requested identity."],[2448,"Verify the result"],[3019,"Verify the result"],[3484,"Verify the result"],[2410,"Verify the result [!toc]"],[2362,"Verify the returned DER:"],[271,"Verify the traffic path"],[1960,"Verify these criteria together:"],[5619,"Version 4.0.0 of the chart is a security-hardening release:"],[4044,"Version compliance and latest versions"],[4179,"Version snapshots, by unique ID"],[4131,"Version snapshots: `.../versions`, `/distribution-sets/:id/versions`"],[57,"Version upgrades."],[245,"Version-specific upgrade steps live in `charts/lamassu/CHANGELOG/` in the Helm repository. The chart also ships an optional pre-upgrade Job that migrates existing CA private keys into the KMS engine configured for the release. It is off by default (`migrations.caToKms.enabled: false`) and is only needed when upgrading from a version prior to 3.7.0."],[4029,"Versions"],[2679,"Versions, compliance and inventory"],[2215,"View details"],[2673,"Vocabulary"],[3814,"Volume"],[5377,"Wait and retry. The lock is released when the operation ends or fails."],[2914,"Wait for the operator's next `rollout` call, or for the devices still in flight"],[3425,"Walk those devices through steps like in step 7, then call `rollout` again for the last device. When nothing is pending or active the launch completes by itself:"],[2508,"We use `Acme Evaluation Root CA` and `device-001` as example names. If you replace them, keep the same client identifier in the steps you want to connect."],[920,"Webhook and combined modes also execute during re-enrollment when configured in its block. They do not implicitly degrade to NO\\_AUTH. An absent or unrecognized mode fails."],[846,"Webhook contract"],[955,"Webhook credentials must be stored as PEM content. File references (`ca_cert_file`, `mtls_options.cert_file`, `mtls_options.key_file`) are never valid in a stored webhook config: delivery rejects any webhook config that references files, whatever its auth mode, so every enroll and reenroll that calls that webhook fails until the certificate and key are entered as Base64-encoded or plain PEM in `mtls_options.cert_content` and `key_content`, or the file fields are removed if the webhook does not use mTLS. If a stored config holds file paths in `mtls_options.cert_content` or `key_content`, every full settings update (`POST /v1/dms`, `PUT /v1/dms/:id`) fails validation — settings are replaced wholesale, so the stored config must be resent — until the values are replaced by PEM content or the webhook stops being used. Meanwhile, delivery of that webhook fails on every call; a metadata-only update through `PUT` or `PATCH` on `/v1/dms/:id/metadata` still succeeds. To fix it in the console, open the DMS, replace any file-path value with PEM content — the editor rejects file paths with an inline field error — and save. Saving rewrites `external_webhook_settings.config` with the current keys, and the console reads both plain and Base64-encoded PEM from migrated configurations and stores the material Base64-encoded. If your stored config previously relied on `validate_server_cert: false`, it now carries `insecure_skip_verify: true`: receiver-certificate verification stays skipped until you set that flag to `false`. See 3.8.0 to 4.0.0 for the upgrade notes and the operator checklist."],[648,"Webhook delivery rules"],[1178,"Webhook denies, times out or responds without authorization"],[5657,"Webhooks with file references"],[2629,"WFX"],[2657,"WFX"],[3311,"WFX"],[3950,"WFX"],[3974,"WFX"],[5145,"WFX"],[2597,"WFX event consumer"],[3256,"WFX event workflow"],[5097,"WFX has a client listener (`8080` in Compose, published as `9080`) and a management listener (`8081`, published as `9081`). Lamassu Updates authenticates neither. Devices need the client listener only: do not expose the management port to devices or untrusted networks."],[3308,"WFX job"],[2967,"WFX, the job server that devices poll in native mode"],[3905,"What"],[4641,"What a client should do"],[3008,"What a device must do to receive updates from Lamassu Updates, in native mode and in hawkBit mode."],[1894,"What a profile controls"],[703,"What an event contains"],[1224,"What cacerts returns"],[2075,"What can be issued"],[5568,"What changes in the 4.0.0 architecture, which changes break a 3.8.0 deployment, how to upgrade a Helm release step by step and how to validate the result."],[5749,"What changes is how API requests are handled. Bodies that still carry `additional_validation_cas` are accepted, but the value is silently dropped because unknown JSON fields are ignored, and GET responses no longer include it. An update body built from the old layout therefore empties `validation_cas` without any error. Always read the DMS before sending an edit and reuse the layout the API returns."],[3097,"What changes with the backend"],[3636,"What differs from native mode"],[80,"What Fastlane adds"],[268,"What if you need three Gateways?"],[3937,"What it connects to"],[2578,"What it does"],[3943,"What it does"],[345,"What it installs"],[2961,"What it is"],[3116,"What it is"],[1749,"What it prepares or checks"],[1544,"What it represents"],[5059,"What Lamassu Updates does and does not protect, and what to put around it before exposing it."],[3746,"What Lamassu Updates does not support or does not do yet, collected in one place with the page that explains each item."],[3632,"What lives where"],[2564,"What runs inside the service"],[1274,"What the connector writes to the shadow"],[2202,"What the KMS solves"],[338,"What the Lamassu chart provides"],[86,"What the script configures"],[5064,"What the service checks"],[3936,"What the service does, how it fits with Lamassu IoT, WFX and hawkBit, and where to start."],[51,"What the service manages"],[5574,"What the upgrade migrates automatically"],[1092,"What to check"],[1169,"What to check"],[3161,"What to do"],[5544,"What to include in a report"],[2530,"What to inspect"],[5061,"What to put in front of it"],[2477,"What to review"],[5201,"What you get"],[1724,"What you manage"],[1978,"What you must maintain"],[2373,"What you need"],[2041,"What you should watch"],[5627,"When `migrations.caToKms.enabled` is set, a pre-upgrade hook registers legacy CA keys in the KMS."],[1423,"When `primary_account` is `false`, the connector registers the CA using `CertificateMode: SNI_ONLY`. This mode requires no verification code or access to the CA's private key, so it is the right choice when the CA is managed from another AWS account or when the administrator does not hold the private key. It is the usual method to register the same CA in several AWS accounts simultaneously."],[2144,"When a CA is deleted, its VA role is kept and cannot be removed through the API. If no other CA with the same SKI remains, the role can be read but any `PUT` returns 404, and the renewal job logs a warning on every cycle because it no longer finds the CA. The API offers no way out; it can only be fixed by editing the database. If another CA with that SKI remains, the role is still editable (see CAs that share a key). Adjust validity and signer before deleting the CA."],[2143,"When a CA is reissued, the role keeps pointing at the previous certificate. CRLs remain valid because both certificates share subject and key, but the previous certificate cannot be deleted while the role uses it. To retire it, update the role’s signer."],[2268,"When an existing PKI needs to be migrated without reissuing certificates."],[3109,"When authentication is on, every call carries the user's bearer token. The service ignores it, so put a gateway that checks it in front. See Security."],[2637,"When building"],[3964,"When building"],[3965,"When building"],[5729,"When CA certificates sharing a `subject_key_id` declare conflicting `engine_id`s, the tool fails that key and exits with code `1`, which fails a Helm pre-upgrade hook. Resolve the conflict manually before rerunning."],[2557,"When escalating a problem, provide identifiers, error and relevant public evidence, including query timestamps and version. This distinguishes issuance, publication and consumer application failures."],[524,"When it allows access, `authz` returns the selected principal in the `X-Principal-ID` response header. The Gateway can propagate that header to the service to preserve the identity context."],[1926,"When it is enabled, Lamassu checks the public key before creating a CA or signing a CSR. A key whose type or size is not listed in the profile is rejected."],[2269,"When Lamassu must operate with a root of trust created by a third party."],[1283,"When several connectors run in parallel, for example for different AWS accounts, each instance has its own `connector_id`. All metadata the connector writes to Lamassu is stored under the key `lamassu.io/iot/{connector_id}`, allowing coexistence without collisions."],[480,"When several principals match"],[2628,"When signing"],[1417,"When the `primary_account` parameter is `true`, the connector runs the full registration flow with proof-of-possession of the private key:"],[3529,"When the build succeeds, the pack becomes `built` and its artifacts and composition are immutable. If it fails, the pack becomes `build_failed` and `last_build_error` holds the reason: fix the input and build again. A build that runs while another one holds the same pack answers `409`."],[1495,"When the connector marks a certificate as `REVOKED` or `INACTIVE` in AWS, it additionally performs a forced disconnect of the device. To do so it opens an MQTT connection over WebSocket (with SigV4 authentication using the connector's credentials) to the AWS IoT Core data endpoint, using the certificate's CN as the MQTT client identifier, and closes it immediately. This terminates any active MQTT session the device may have at that moment."],[1462,"When the device establishes its first MQTT connection to AWS IoT Core presenting the Lamassu-issued certificate, AWS detects that the certificate belongs to a CA registered with a JITP template, executes that template and creates the Thing automatically. The initial connection may fail or be delayed while the template runs; firmware must be prepared to retry the connection."],[1091,"When the device exists"],[5744,"When the legacy mTLS certificate and key are both PEM, they move into `mtls_options.cert_content` and `mtls_options.key_content` and need no action. Two cases do need it."],[5756,"When the request `Accept` header includes `text/event-stream`, the events endpoint streams over SSE with a keepalive every 15 seconds, and returns `501` when SSE is disabled in the deployment. Each Device Manager instance binds a durable AMQP queue named after its service ID, `device-manager-sse-<uuid>`. Queues from crashed or replaced instances linger until removed, so configure an expiration or cleanup policy on the broker."],[3339,"When the service publishes the device artifact URL (the `ddi_url` path), it serves that same route itself, for modules in the device's assigned or installed set only, and with S3 presigning enabled it answers `302` to a short-lived signed URL. A device does not need to know the difference: it follows the redirect."],[1765,"When to choose each operation"],[1977,"When to use it"],[616,"When you edit a shared policy, remember the change affects all its principals. To test a new scope, create a separate policy, grant it to a test identity and validate allowed and denied operations before replacing the old one."],[2267,"When you have an exportable private key and the destination engine supports import."],[2290,"When you open it, two areas appear: **Sign** and **Verify**."],[2310,"When you open the form, it asks for the subject data and the main certificate attributes:"],[76,"When you receive your instance's credentials, continue with Get started."],[3065,"When your gateway routes hawkBit's device artifact URLs (the path of `hawkbit.ddi_url`, `.../<tenant>/controller/v1/<controller>/softwaremodules/<id>/artifacts/<file>`) to Lamassu Updates, the service serves them only for modules assigned to or installed on that controller, and answers `404` otherwise. With presigned downloads on, it redirects to a signed URL of hawkBit's S3 bucket instead of streaming the file."],[1370,"Where in this page"],[3090,"Where things are"],[2566,"Where things are stored"],[3035,"Where to download the deliverable. The service appends the device ID, so each device fetches its own file. A `.swu` for SWU packs, a `.tar.gz` for `non-swu` packs"],[3939,"Where to go next"],[5043,"Where uploads and builds write temporary files (Go's temporary directory)."],[4328,"Whether its deliverable exists, and whether it is frozen because a version of it has shipped"],[4308,"Whether older versions can be downloaded"],[5260,"Which devices use a key"],[4744,"Which keys each mode reads"],[2809,"Which pack version a device currently carries"],[246,"While the flag is enabled, every `helm upgrade` creates the `<chart fullname>-ca-to-kms-migration` Job (`lamassu-ca-to-kms-migration` for release `lamassu`); a fresh install does not create it. Its hook weight is `-10`, so it runs before the other pre-upgrade hooks: the authorization bootstrap configuration (weight `-4`) and the database-migration Job (weight `-3`)."],[2077,"Who accepts the result"],[2074,"Who can issue"],[2076,"Who can request it"],[3728,"Who devices poll"],[5630,"Who is affected"],[30,"Who operates the infrastructure"],[1539,"Who prepares each part"],[3307,"Who tells the device"],[3917,"Why"],[5100,"Why"],[4312,"Why the last build failed"],[1919,"With **Honor Extensions**, Lamassu filters the additional requested extensions and keeps only SAN. Without that option, it discards the CSR's additional extensions."],[1918,"With **Honor Subject**, the certificate keeps the subject of the CSR. Without that option, Lamassu applies the profile's `CN`, `O`, `OU`, `C`, `ST` and `L`. If the profile does not define a `Common Name`, the requested CN is kept."],[179,"With `--development`, the fixed `lamassu`/`lamassu` password grants `pki-admin` access to anyone who can reach the installation. Never use it on anything exposed beyond a development machine or a network-reachable host."],[5149,"With `app.logs.level: debug` the service logs the whole configuration, secrets included, and in `native` mode it prints the `storage` block (with its password) to standard output at every start. Use `info` or higher in any environment whose logs are stored."],[5049,"With `app.logs.level: debug`, the service writes the whole loaded configuration to the log at start-up, including `storage.password`, `encryption.local_key`, S3 keys and `hawkbit.password`. In `native` mode the `storage` block, password included, is also printed to standard output at every start, whatever the log level. Use `info` or higher anywhere logs are stored or shared, and keep secrets in a secret store, not in the file (see the `*_file` credentials above)."],[3343,"With `auto` off, hawkBit pauses after each group. Each `rollout` call releases the next one:"],[2884,"With `auto` on, one `rollout` call starts the launch and the following batches are released by flow 4. A launch with `scheduled_at` is started by the scheduled-launch trigger on the leader replica once that time passes."],[3429,"With `auto` on, the service sends the next batch itself once enough of the current one succeeded. `approval_threshold` is the percentage of the batch that must succeed (0 or 100 mean all of it). `error_threshold` applies to every launch, `auto` or not: the launch is cancelled once the number of failed devices reaches that percentage of **all** devices, rounded up. With 4 devices at 75%, the third failure cancels it."],[851,"With `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE`, the device presents its initial certificate and proves key possession through TLS. The DMS validates it against admission CAs. In the inspected flow, the validator receives one leaf credential: configure and test the direct issuer for the simple case; sending more intermediates does not establish delivery to the service."],[3716,"With `dms_endpoint` set, every read of a group's devices creates the missing targets (controller ID = device ID) and tags them `group:<id>`. A device that cannot be reconciled is excluded from that read and logged. Without `dms_endpoint`, groups are the existing `group:<id>` tags."],[5125,"With `presign.enabled`, a download is answered `302` to a short-lived URL of the S3 store. The service authorizes the caller first (the gateway for the static route and, in `hawkbit` mode, the device's assignment); the store then checks only the signature, so **the URL is a bearer capability**: whoever holds it can fetch that one object until it expires. The design keeps that acceptable:"],[2155,"With `regenerate_on_revoke`, the event handler requests a new CRL upon revocation or reactivation from `CertificateHold` to `ACTIVE`. This happens after the status change and has its own failure paths: if it fails (missing role, invalid signer), the event is retried and, once retries are exhausted, moves to the failed-events queue. Confirming revocation does not establish that the new CRL is already published or that all clients have downloaded it."],[5717,"With `tls.certManagerOptions.issuer` empty, the chart creates a release-scoped self-signed issuer, for example `lamassu-downstream-ca-selfsigned-issuer`. The legacy literal `downstream-ca-selfsigned-issuer` is still accepted and resolves to it."],[192,"With both `--tls-crt` and `--tls-key`, Fastlane creates the `downstream-provided-crt` Secret and configures `tls.type: external`."],[1145,"With certificate authentication, review `enable_expired_renewal` and whether Gateway permits presenting that credential. Revocation, subject and the remaining checks still apply."],[953,"With credentials configured, they are sent regardless of the URL scheme: an `http://` webhook receives the API key or bearer token in cleartext, and mTLS has no effect because no TLS handshake takes place, so the client certificate is never presented. Prefer `https://` for `url`, `oidc_well_known` and the token endpoint it advertises."],[5730,"With Helm, enable the hook for the upgrade in step 2; without Helm, run the tool by hand."],[1003,"With initial `CLIENT_CERTIFICATE` authentication, send the Base64-encoded DER CSR using `Content-Type: application/pkcs10`:"],[3896,"With Leases, one Lease per role is created in the pod's namespace (`lamassu-updates-wfx-event-consumer` and `lamassu-updates-scheduled-launch-trigger`), and `kubectl get lease` shows the pod that leads (`POD_NAME` is the holder). The lease lasts 15 s and is renewed within 10 s, so a failover takes about that long. With the database lock, a standby retries every 15 s and the leader verifies every 5 s that its connection is still alive."],[3773,"With local storage on one `ReadWriteOnce` volume, only one `native` replica can run. Move `artifacts`, `builds` and `symkms` to S3 to scale out. See Operations."],[232,"With no CA Secret configured, the event-bus clients use the system-trusted CAs. To trust a private CA, create a Secret in the release namespace containing the CA certificate in PEM format and set `amqp.ca.secretName` (and `amqp.ca.certificateKey` if the data key is not `ca.crt`)."],[4977,"With no credential set, the AWS default chain applies (environment, IRSA, instance profile)."],[5780,"With release `lamassu` and the default chart name, the new PVCs are `golang-engine-storage-lamassu-kms-0` and `local-crl-file-storage-lamassu-va-0`; with release `prod` they are `golang-engine-storage-prod-lamassu-kms-0` and `local-crl-file-storage-prod-lamassu-va-0`. Confirm both old and new PVCs exist before continuing. If `claims_ok` prints `ABORT`, the block stops before listing the claims: either a StatefulSet lookup failed, or a new claim resolves to the 3.8.0 claim. In the second case the copy would verify against itself and the final cleanup would delete the live volume. The chart always appends `-kms` and `-va` to the release-scoped name, so a collision means the claims are not what this guide expects; investigate before going on. Item 3 runs the same check and refuses to create the helper pod, so nothing touches the volumes. The check returns instead of calling `exit`, which would close your shell and lose the variables set so far. If you only migrate one of the two services, drop the other one's claims from the commands that follow."],[4099,"With S3 storage and presigning enabled, a `GET` can answer `302` to a signed URL of the object store, with `Cache-Control: no-store`. The caller is authorized first; the store then only checks the signature. A `HEAD` is never redirected. Native mode signs build outputs (`.swu` and packages); hawkBit mode signs the objects of hawkBit's S3 artifact plugin. If signing fails, the service streams the file instead."],[4994,"With S3 storage, a download can be answered with `302` to a short-lived signed URL of the store instead of streaming the bytes through the service. The caller is authorized first, as for any download. Native mode signs build outputs (`builds.presign`); `hawkbit` mode signs the objects of hawkBit's S3 plugin (`hawkbit.artifact_store.presign`). Artifact downloads by ID in `native` mode are always streamed."],[2883,"With the `phased` workflow a job starts in `CREATED`, and some transitions need a person or system to confirm them in WFX. This service does not advance them. The `download-install` workflow, used by `non-swu` packs, runs `DOWNLOAD → DOWNLOADING → DOWNLOADED → INSTALL → INSTALLING → INSTALLED`."],[3723,"With the plugin and `hawkbit.artifact_store.presign` configured, downloads of module artifacts are answered `302` to a short-lived signed URL of the bucket instead of streaming through hawkBit or this service. Devices keep the artifact link hawkBit gave them: the adapter serves that one DDI route (`<ddi path>/:tenant/controller/v1/:controllerId/softwaremodules/:id/artifacts/:filename`), checks that the module is assigned to or installed on that device (otherwise `404`) and redirects. For this to work, the gateway must route that path to Lamassu Updates and the rest of the DDI to hawkBit. See Configuration and Security."],[1638,"With the same server still running, test that a request without a client certificate is rejected:"],[3556,"Without `auto`, each call dispatches the next batch (the canary first, if you set one). With `auto: true`, call it once to start the first batch: the service then releases the later batches itself as the approval threshold is met, and further calls answer `400`. If the launch has a future `scheduled_at`, the call is accepted but does nothing: the service starts it by itself at that time (native checks every 30 seconds; hawkBit's own scheduler starts the rollout)."],[3439,"Without `workflow`, a `swu` pack uses `phased`. Only the pack's current version can be forced, unless the pack was created with `allow_previous_version_download`."],[2878,"Without signing fields the manifest is stored unsigned. If a signing key is given but no certificate, the build fails instead."],[5791,"Without the chart's database job, no tool moves data between databases: the migration tooling only applies migrations to a schema that already exists. Move each per-service database into its `pki` schema yourself, preserving structure, indexes, constraints, data and migration history, with writes stopped and a verified backup in hand."],[193,"Without them, it configures cert-manager with a self-signed issuer. This is suitable for a lab but will not be trusted by clients until you distribute the CA. Use Helm when you need a corporate Issuer, public ACME issuer or an existing Secret managed by another system."],[343,"Workflow"],[3044,"Workflow"],[2761,"Workflow file"],[2717,"Workflow terminal state"],[4155,"Workflows registered in WFX, as `{\"content\": [...]}`. Empty in hawkBit mode"],[3912,"Written to standard output: the application log (level `app.logs.level`) and an access log of every request. At `debug` the start-up log includes the whole configuration, secrets too."],[5340,"Wrong bucket, endpoint or credentials, or the bucket does not exist"],[439,"wrong credentials or DNS for PostgreSQL or RabbitMQ;"],[5433,"Wrong key ID or `user`, or the endpoint points at another instance"],[479,"X.509 principals"],[1317,"Yes"],[1321,"Yes"],[1325,"Yes"],[1349,"Yes"],[1353,"Yes"],[1357,"Yes"],[1361,"Yes"],[2583,"Yes"],[2587,"Yes"],[2591,"Yes"],[2595,"Yes"],[2599,"Yes"],[2603,"Yes"],[2607,"Yes"],[2611,"Yes"],[4481,"Yes"],[4484,"Yes"],[4487,"Yes"],[4490,"Yes"],[4664,"Yes"],[4665,"Yes"],[4667,"Yes"],[4668,"Yes"],[4670,"Yes"],[4671,"Yes"],[4673,"Yes"],[4676,"Yes"],